### Health check
GET http://localhost:8080/api/test

### Sign up (register)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "John Doe",
  "email": "john@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}

### Sign in (login)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "john@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}

### Sign in with wrong password (should fail)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "john@example.com",
  "password": "wrongpassword"
}

### Sign up with duplicate email (should fail)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Jane Doe",
  "email": "john@example.com",
  "password": "password456"
}

### Sign in and use token (copy token from sign-in response to below)
GET http://localhost:8080/api/test
Authorization: Bearer <token>

### Sign out is client-side only (removes token from localStorage)
### No backend endpoint needed — just discard the token on the frontend.
### To verify sign-out behavior, try using the old token after logout:

GET http://localhost:8080/api/test
Authorization: Bearer <old-token>
### → still returns 200 because the backend is stateless.
### For full invalidation, add a token blacklist on the backend.

### H2 Console (only if using H2, not PostgreSQL)
GET http://localhost:8080/h2-console
